{"absent":["Full definitions, widths and alignment of atomic_t and atomic64_t beyond the observed counter member.","Implementations and hardware guarantees of the arch exchange, compare-exchange, add, load and store primitives.","Semantics and scope of the smp ordered accesses, conditional loads and before and after atomic barriers.","Spurious-failure, ABA and failure-ordering behavior of compare-exchange beyond the success plus refreshed-expectation contract.","Routed-subset gaps such as sub, and, or, xor, dec, inc and test operations and remaining fetch and add-return orderings.","Locking, interrupt, preemption or signal safety of these primitives beyond memory ordering."],"dossier":"D03","findings":[{"kind":"export","sids":["include/linux/atomic.h::atomic_cond_read_acquire","include/linux/atomic.h::atomic_cond_read_relaxed","include/linux/atomic.h::atomic64_cond_read_acquire","include/linux/atomic.h::atomic64_cond_read_relaxed"],"text":"Polling conditional reads exist for standard and 64-bit atomics in acquire and relaxed orderings. A caller provides an object plus a predicate over its observed value and the read repeats against the stored counter until the predicate accepts."},{"kind":"invariant","sids":["include/linux/atomic.h::__atomic_acquire_fence","include/linux/atomic.h::__atomic_release_fence","include/linux/atomic.h::__atomic_pre_full_fence","include/linux/atomic.h::__atomic_post_full_fence"],"text":"Ordering helpers delegate to the underlying atomic barriers. Acquire and post-full use the after-atomic barrier and release and pre-full use the before-atomic barrier, fixing which surrounding accesses cannot migrate across the atomic operation."},{"kind":"invariant","sids":["include/linux/atomic.h::__atomic_op_acquire","include/linux/atomic.h::__atomic_op_release","include/linux/atomic.h::__atomic_op_fence"],"text":"Three generic constructors synthesize stronger orderings from a relaxed architecture operation of the same shape. One provides acquire semantics for a value-returning operation, one provides release semantics for a non-value-returning operation, and one provides full two-sided ordering for a value-returning operation."},{"kind":"export","sids":["include/linux/atomic/atomic-arch-fallback.h::raw_xchg.alt_1","include/linux/atomic/atomic-arch-fallback.h::raw_xchg.alt_2","include/linux/atomic/atomic-arch-fallback.h::raw_xchg.alt_3","include/linux/atomic/atomic-arch-fallback.h::raw_xchg_acquire.alt_1","include/linux/atomic/atomic-arch-fallback.h::raw_xchg_acquire.alt_2","include/linux/atomic/atomic-arch-fallback.h::raw_xchg_acquire.alt_3","include/linux/atomic/atomic-arch-fallback.h::raw_xchg_acquire.alt_4","include/linux/atomic/atomic-arch-fallback.h::raw_xchg_release.alt_1","include/linux/atomic/atomic-arch-fallback.h::raw_xchg_release.alt_2","include/linux/atomic/atomic-arch-fallback.h::raw_xchg_release.alt_3","include/linux/atomic/atomic-arch-fallback.h::raw_xchg_release.alt_4","include/linux/atomic/atomic-arch-fallback.h::raw_xchg_relaxed.alt_1","include/linux/atomic/atomic-arch-fallback.h::raw_xchg_relaxed.alt_2","include/linux/atomic/atomic-arch-fallback.h::raw_xchg_relaxed.alt_3"],"text":"Raw unconditional exchange is offered in full, acquire, release and relaxed orderings. Selection prefers a dedicated architecture ordering variant, otherwise builds ordering around the plain architecture exchange with the generic constructors or reuses the plain operation where allowed."},{"kind":"export","sids":["include/linux/atomic/atomic-arch-fallback.h::raw_cmpxchg.alt_1","include/linux/atomic/atomic-arch-fallback.h::raw_cmpxchg.alt_2","include/linux/atomic/atomic-arch-fallback.h::raw_cmpxchg.alt_3","include/linux/atomic/atomic-arch-fallback.h::raw_cmpxchg_acquire.alt_1","include/linux/atomic/atomic-arch-fallback.h::raw_cmpxchg_acquire.alt_3","include/linux/atomic/atomic-arch-fallback.h::raw_cmpxchg_release.alt_1","include/linux/atomic/atomic-arch-fallback.h::raw_cmpxchg_release.alt_2","include/linux/atomic/atomic-